<PublicationLayout
  category="Research"
  type="Research note"
  title="Why a statistical method name is not enough"
  description="Primary-source review showed why procedure names alone cannot identify statistical guarantees, assumptions, comparison families, or later variants."
  published="September 1, 2026"
  status="Source-bounded research finding — independently checked, not peer reviewed; FND-1 gate open"
  toc={toc}
>
  <section id="current-status">
    <h2>Current status</h2>
    <div class="notice">
      <p>
        <strong>
          This is a source-bounded research note, not a peer-reviewed paper and not a
          Protocol decision.
        </strong>
      </p>
      <p>
        The completed findings passed independent close review. The wider FND-1
        research gate remains open, and no statistical procedure has been selected for
        nomue Protocol.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="finding">
    <h2>The finding</h2>
    <p>
      A familiar procedure name is not enough to identify what a statistical result
      guarantees. The meaning also depends on which claims belong to the comparison
      family, which error quantity is controlled, the theorem's assumptions, sidedness,
      balance conditions, and the exact procedure variant.
    </p>
    <p>
      This matters because closely associated names can refer to different questions.
      Controlling the probability of any false rejection in a family is not the same as
      controlling the expected proportion of false discoveries. Comparing every group
      with one control is not the same as comparing every pair. A later extension also
      cannot inherit the evidence for an earlier procedure merely by sharing its name.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="primary-sources">
    <h2>What the primary sources establish</h2>
    <p>
      The investigation inspected the primary texts for Holm's sequentially rejective
      procedure, the original Benjamini–Hochberg false-discovery-rate procedure,
      Dunnett's many-to-one construction, and Tukey's 1949 procedure. The review kept
      their claims separate rather than treating the four names as interchangeable
      labels for “multiple comparisons.”
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Holm's main 1979 result gives strong family-wise error control using valid
        marginal test levels and the union bound. A separate product-form construction
        in the same paper uses an independence assumption.
      </li>
      <li>
        The 1995 Benjamini–Hochberg theorem is an independence result about false
        discovery rate. Later results for other dependence structures are not supplied
        by that paper.
      </li>
      <li>
        Dunnett's 1955 construction addresses many-to-one comparisons. Its numerical
        tables have recorded limits involving balance, sidedness, variance assumptions,
        and approximation.
      </li>
      <li>
        Tukey's 1949 paper describes a three-stage gap, straggler, and upper-tail F
        procedure. It does not establish the later Studentized-range all-pairs procedure
        or its unequal-size extension.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="all-pairs">
    <h2>Separating later all-pairs sources</h2>
    <p>
      A follow-up investigation examined later sources commonly associated with
      all-pairs comparisons. It found that their evidential roles must also remain
      separate.
    </p>
    <div class="table-scroll">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Source</th>
            <th>Source-bounded role</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Hayter (1984)</td>
            <td>
              Later-primary formal support for the balanced Studentized-range
              construction and a conservativeness result for Tukey–Kramer under the
              recorded conditions
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Kramer (1956)</td>
            <td>
              A proposal and illustration for unequal replication, not the later general
              proof
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Spjøtvoll and Stoline (1973)</td>
            <td>A distinct extended T-method, not another name for Tukey–Kramer</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Dunnett (1980)</td>
            <td>Simulation and comparison evidence, not the general proof</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      The authenticated text of Tukey's 1953 manuscript or its 1994 archival printing
      was not inspected. Exact historical attribution to that manuscript therefore
      remains open even though the later mathematical and source-role questions above
      have been narrowed and closed.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="limits">
    <h2>Limits</h2>
    <p>
      Independent close reviews checked the frozen reports, their source-claim
      boundaries, and the repository evidence. Some inspected source PDFs could not be
      redistributed in the public repository and were unavailable to the close-review
      environments. The review records preserve that access limitation instead of
      reconstructing or broadening the source claims.
    </p>
    <p>
      This note does not compare the operating performance of the procedures, recommend
      a default, verify a software implementation, or show that one error criterion is
      universally preferable. Results remain limited to the cited editions, theorem
      conditions, and source roles recorded in the investigation.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="protocol-relation">
    <h2>Relation to nomue Protocol</h2>
    <p>
      The finding is research input for future identity and interpretation design. It
      does not add a Record field, vocabulary term, method identifier, schema, public
      check, supported procedure, or implementation behavior.
    </p>
    <p>
      Three of eight recorded FND-1 holds are closed in narrowed form. Five holds remain
      open, alongside the historical-attribution residual described above. The complete
      FND-1 research gate is therefore still open, and adopting any result would require
      a separate Protocol proposal and review.
    </p>
  </section>

